# ------------------------------------------------------------------ discussion
H("Discussion")
P(f"In 1.7 million articles of three large publishers, acceptance that was unusually fast for the same journal and year was followed by retraction for peer-review-related reasons about four times as often as in the rest of the same journal-year, the relation was graded, specific to the editorial process, absent for a negative-control exposure and not explained by a few journals. This agrees in direction with, and extends to individual articles, the editor-level and special-issue-level observations made for Hindawi and PLOS ONE ⟦bishop2023_hindawi_si,richardson2025_pnas⟧. The journal-level null result of a recent study in this journal ⟦mascato2025_ripr⟧ is not in conflict: our ecological analysis also found no positive relation for the share of very fast acceptances, and the signal is relative to the journal's own norm. Our finding that absolute speed (within 7 days) and the accepted-to-online lag carry no signal once journal is accounted for suggests that tools based on fixed thresholds will flag many fast-lane articles without benefit.")
P("The size of the effect and its practical value are different questions. The odds ratio is large, but retractions are rare, so flagged articles are overwhelmingly not retracted: even in the fastest 1% only about one in two hundred articles was later retracted for process-related reasons, and the score identified a small share of such retractions. Timing is therefore not a detector of misconduct. A reasonable use, consistent with how publishers describe their own integrity screening, is to prioritise journals, special issues or editors for editorial-office review when a cluster of unusually fast acceptances occurs, in combination with other signals, and never to infer anything about a single paper. The decline of the association in articles accepted in 2023 and 2024, which survives equal follow-up, suggests either that the manipulation that produced fast acceptances in 2020–2022 has become less common or less detectable by timing, or that retractions for other reasons now dominate; screening rules should therefore be re-estimated routinely rather than fixed.")
P("The study also illustrates two pitfalls for work in this area. Pooling across journals and years, or comparing retracted with all other articles, would confound speed with the journal's practices and follow-up; the ecological association of very fast articles and retraction even has the opposite sign to the within-journal one. And because a handful of journals contribute most events, associations such as that for batch acceptance can vanish when mass-retraction events are removed, so conclusions should always be checked for concentration.")
H("Limitations")
for t in ["Observational design: associations are conditional on journal and year; they do not show that fast acceptance caused inadequate review, and we cannot observe the review itself.",
          "Outcome: retraction is a late, incomplete and publisher-dependent marker; unretracted problematic articles are counted as controls, which would bias the odds ratio towards one if they are also fast; Retraction Watch labels are revised over time ⟦singh2026_rw_revisions⟧ and the snapshot does not record when each notice entered the database.",
          f"Generality: the data are three publishers whose journals deposit received and accepted dates; Taylor & Francis contributed {T['by_member']['Taylor & Francis']['retracted']} retractions, so results speak for Springer Nature and Wiley journals; Wiley's odds ratio was smaller and its interval included one; the external replication had too few events to be informative.",
          "Concentration and time: most events come from few journals and from articles accepted in 2020–2022; the association was absent in 2024 articles.",
          "Exposure: duration is publisher-defined and, for resubmitted or transferred manuscripts, may not measure the editorial process; article type is not observed, so fast-lane items are not separated; the out-of-time score uses the cohort-shift variance learned in an earlier cycle of this project, and a simple within-year percentile performed as well.",
          "Multiplicity and post hoc analyses: only two tests were primary; amendments responding to the first results are disclosed and should be read as exploratory.",
          "Process: the analysis and first drafts were produced by an AI system with automated reviews and no human expert review before submission; the authors have re-examined the results, and the code and data are available for independent replication."]:
    p = doc.add_paragraph(cite_sub(t), style="List Bullet"); p.paragraph_format.space_after = Pt(3)
H("Conclusions")
P("Acceptance that is unusually fast relative to the same journal and year is associated with later retraction for peer-review-related reasons, with a graded relation, specificity to such reasons and robustness to mass-retraction events, but only in articles accepted before 2024 and mainly in a small number of journals. As a screen it is weak because most flagged articles are not retracted. Journal-relative timing, not absolute speed, should be the basis for any editorial-office monitoring, which should prioritise review of journals and special issues and never be used to judge individual papers.")
